<DocsLayout title="CI and reports" description="Use VulnWeave reports and reviewed gates in local workflows and CI.">
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">GUIDE</p><h1>Reports, gates, and CI</h1><p class="lede">Start locally, review the baseline, and then use the same commands in CI. A failed gate means “review is required,” not that the scanner or command itself is broken.</p>
    <h2>Local HTML report</h2><pre><code>pnpm cli -- report . &gt; vulnweave-report.html</code></pre><p>The report is a self-contained file. It includes severity totals, scanner health, canonical findings, priorities, remediation guidance, evidence graph totals, maintainability sections, and local scan trends. It does not upload source or scan data.</p>
    <h2>SARIF export</h2><pre><code>pnpm build&#10;node packages/cli/dist/index.js . --format sarif &gt; vulnweave.sarif</code></pre><p>SARIF 2.1.0 can be uploaded to compatible code-scanning systems. The repository workflow exports it as an artifact even when a policy gate fails.</p>
    <h2>Gates</h2><pre><code>pnpm cli -- ci .&#10;pnpm cli -- . --format summary --fail-on high --require-analyzers&#10;pnpm cli -- . --format summary --baseline latest --fail-on high&#10;pnpm cli -- . --format summary --baseline-file vulnweave.findings-baseline.json --fail-on high</code></pre><p>A standard gate evaluates findings at or above the chosen severity. A baseline gate evaluates only new findings since the selected baseline. Use <code>--baseline</code> for a local saved run, or commit a baseline JSON created by <code>vulnweave baseline .</code> and use <code>--baseline-file</code> in CI. Analyzer health is a separate condition: use <code>--require-analyzers</code> when every selected scanner must complete.</p>
    <h2>Reusable GitHub audit</h2><p>Public repositories can call the bundled reusable workflow. It checks out the target repository, runs the four local analyzers, applies the selected gate, and uploads an HTML report even when the gate needs review.</p><pre><code>jobs:&#10;  vulnweave:&#10;    uses: KumarSanthosh16/vulnweave/.github/workflows/reusable-audit.yml@main&#10;    with:&#10;      fail-on: high&#10;      baseline-file: vulnweave.findings-baseline.json</code></pre><p>Omit <code>baseline-file</code> to gate all findings. When present, it must be a reviewed relative path in the calling repository, and the gate evaluates only newly introduced findings. After the v0.2 release, pin the workflow to its reviewed release tag or commit SHA rather than <code>main</code>. The calling repository needs only read access to its contents.</p>
    <h2>GitHub Actions remediation summary</h2><p>Every bundled workflow writes a compact VulnWeave summary to the run page, even when the security gate fails. It lists the gate and analyzer-health result, changed-code scope when used, the highest-priority active remediation work, and any accepted risks with their owner and expiry. It is derived from the saved local scan record; it does not run another scan or change project files. The HTML report and SARIF remain workflow artifacts for deeper investigation.</p>
    <h2>Trends, plans, and remediation</h2><pre><code>pnpm cli -- history .&#10;pnpm cli -- . --format upgrades&#10;pnpm cli -- . --format remediation</code></pre><p>All three are advisory. Upgrade plans show only scanner-reported fixed versions, identify the reported manifest, and give ecosystem-aware next steps for Python requirements and npm manifests. Remediation never edits code, lockfiles, configuration, or credentials.</p>
  </article>
